WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.420
You ask AI for a plan,

2
00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:03.320
then ask it to review its own

3
00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:04.420
work.

4
00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:05.780
It approves it.

5
00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:06.980
Every time.

6
00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:08.640
The model that wrote the plan,

7
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.320
especially in the same session,

8
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.520
tends to think everything is fine.

9
00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:13.460
I’m Nei Maldaner,

10
00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:15.680
and the solution is simple:

11
00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.580
one plans, the other critiques.

12
00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:20.960
Claude and Codex complement each other when

13
00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:23.180
each handles one part and the other

14
00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:25.020
checks the actual artifact.

15
00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:25.900
In this video,

16
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.540
I’ll show you how to use them

17
00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:31.400
together and everything INEMA already has ready:

18
00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:34.500
courses, kits, and tools,

19
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:35.440
all

20
00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:36.140
open.

21
00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.940
The foundation is Mark Kashef’s Use Both

22
00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.920
kit, with an open license.

23
00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:43.340
It organizes work with both tools across

24
00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:45.060
six levels, with

25
00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:47.200
ready-to-use prompts and two small skills,

26
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:48.320
handoff and prime,

27
00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:50.640
so you can switch assistants without explaining

28
00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:51.580
the project again.

29
00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:54.180
And one rule applies at every level:

30
00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:56.520
the second assistant gets the same brief

31
00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.200
and the real artifact.

32
00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.640
The file, the diff,

33
00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:00.440
the image.

34
00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.460
Never a vague summary of what the

35
00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:03.900
first one did.

36
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:05.960
Level one: let the two discuss.

37
00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:07.920
One writes the plan,

38
00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.940
the other critiques the same file,

39
00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:13.480
read-only, with evidence and severity.

40
00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:15.280
At most two rounds,

41
00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:18.600
because two AIs agreeing isn’t proof.

42
00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.300
Level two, optional:

43
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:23.120
generate images, only if there’s an image

44
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:23.940
tool in the session.

45
00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.360
Done means the real file is open

46
00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.520
at its intended size.

47
00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:31.540
Level three: split building and reviewing.

48
00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:33.300
One builds on a branch,

49
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.340
the other reviews the diff against the

50
00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.180
brief and tests.

51
00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:39.720
The tests and you decide what goes

52
00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:40.120
in.

53
00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:41.210
Level

54
00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:43.420
four: supervised computer use.

55
00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.440
The assistant prepares an action in an

56
00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:48.340
interface and stops before submitting it.

57
00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:51.460
Passwords, payments, and important submissions stay with

58
00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:51.640
you.

59
00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:52.840
Level

60
00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.020
five: a goal with a stopping condition.

61
00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.940
Define observable success,

62
00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.480
which tests to run,

63
00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:00.340
what output to expect,

64
00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:01.800
and limits on files,

65
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.200
rounds, and spend.

66
00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:06.160
Asking it to stop in two hours

67
00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:07.380
is a request,

68
00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:08.620
not a limit.

69
00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:11.600
Level six: handoff and prime.

70
00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:14.080
Handoff saves the state in a portable

71
00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.620
file, and prime makes the next assistant

72
00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:19.540
read and verify that state before continuing.

73
00:02:19.574 --> 00:02:22.154
And here’s everything available to you,

74
00:02:22.154 --> 00:02:23.454
all open.

75
00:02:23.454 --> 00:02:24.904
Six courses:

76
00:02:24.904 --> 00:02:26.274
Claude para

77
00:02:26.274 --> 00:02:28.114
Codex, in Portuguese,

78
00:02:28.114 --> 00:02:29.154
English,

79
00:02:29.154 --> 00:02:30.834
and Spanish;

80
00:02:30.834 --> 00:02:34.374
Codex Básico, the complete Codex CLI course;

81
00:02:34.374 --> 00:02:37.794
Master Codex, the SaaS factory with agents;

82
00:02:37.794 --> 00:02:40.454
iClaudeX, planning with Claude and Codex in

83
00:02:40.454 --> 00:02:41.664
the terminal;

84
00:02:41.664 --> 00:02:44.914
MakeClaudeX, from prompt to production plugin;

85
00:02:44.914 --> 00:02:46.114
and

86
00:02:46.425 --> 00:02:48.800
DeepClaudeX, multi model,

87
00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.174
seventy, twenty, ten.

88
00:02:51.174 --> 00:02:52.494
Plus the kits:

89
00:02:52.494 --> 00:02:53.401
agente

90
00:02:53.401 --> 00:02:55.834
claude codex, to migrate or stay agnostic;

91
00:02:55.834 --> 00:02:58.354
Use Both, with the six levels;

92
00:02:58.354 --> 00:02:59.794
the claudex CLI;

93
00:02:59.794 --> 00:03:01.334
and Codex Cheat Sheet,

94
00:03:01.334 --> 00:03:02.394
the quick reference.

95
00:03:02.394 --> 00:03:05.014
Everything has a GitHub repository.

96
00:03:05.014 --> 00:03:06.814
Where should you start?

97
00:03:06.814 --> 00:03:08.214
Choose a small task.

98
00:03:08.214 --> 00:03:10.654
Write the brief and acceptance

99
00:03:10.654 --> 00:03:11.614
criteria.

100
00:03:11.614 --> 00:03:14.194
Ask one to make the plan and

101
00:03:14.194 --> 00:03:15.394
the other to critique it,

102
00:03:15.394 --> 00:03:16.674
using the same file,

103
00:03:16.674 --> 00:03:18.774
for at most two rounds.

104
00:03:18.774 --> 00:03:20.374
Before ending the session,

105
00:03:20.374 --> 00:03:21.454
do the handoff.

106
00:03:21.454 --> 00:03:23.524
You’ll find all this in the Codex

107
00:03:23.524 --> 00:03:25.994
plus Claude section at eventos

108
00:03:25.994 --> 00:03:27.054
dot inema dot pro,

109
00:03:27.054 --> 00:03:28.054
slash

110
00:03:28.054 --> 00:03:30.214
codex claude, in Portuguese,

111
00:03:30.214 --> 00:03:31.954
English, and Spanish.

112
00:03:31.954 --> 00:03:33.974
Open content from INEMA.CLUB.
